"""Packed fixture data for `clean_canary.py` -- deliberately its own module.

Split out from `clean_canary.py` (PR #579 round-2 review): every snippet here is
stored as a SINGLE PHYSICAL LINE with escaped newlines, which is the exact packing that made
the original combined module unreadable as ordinary source -- the self-audit's own guards
collapse against it (a line-window guard sees a whole multi-line snippet as one line; a
file-wide provenance gate gets force-opened by an unrelated neighbouring plant; a
same-function-block heuristic has no column-0 boundary between ~250 concatenated entries),
producing structurally-forced false positives that are not present when the snippets are
scanned individually. The count is deliberately NOT pinned here: it grows with the fixture
set and with every new guard, it was written as 9 and reached 10 with nothing watching, and
`scripts/doc_counts.py` does not scan this file. `agentsec doctor --clean-canary` is the
live oracle -- it writes each snippet to its own file and proves the guards stay silent.

Keeping ONLY the data half in this module lets the self-audit exclude it narrowly
(`tests/test_self_audit.py`'s `SELF_AUDIT_EXCLUDES`, which the ci.yml dogfood step is held to
by `test_dogfood_ci_step_carries_the_same_exclusion`) without also blinding `clean_canary.py`'s
real pipeline (`write_clean_canary_repo`, `compare_clean`, `run_clean_canary`, `to_text`),
which ships in the wheel and executes on a user's machine -- the file-wide exclusion the
original module used covered both, so a genuine vulnerability planted in the pipeline half
could never again be surfaced by the self-audit.
"""
